WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Welcome to a new week on Tesla Short's Time Daily episode 442.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.260
It's April 20th, 2026, and I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.840
Let's see what the new week has in store for Tesla.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.240
Full self-driving is now live on Teslas in Europe with firsthand test drives starting to surface.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.540
Tesla has started offering autonomous taxi rides in Dallas and Houston.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.380
The network is still very small, according to early reports.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.740
You might have to hunt around for one of the first two vehicles in service.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:32.640
A passenger recently said his robot taxi missed a turn and tried to pull over in an unsafe spot on the highway.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.560
Incidents like this illustrate the edge cases the system still encounters in real traffic.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.800
Morgan Stanley called the move tangible progress ahead of earnings.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.420
That stands out because the firm has often been skeptical on timelines.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.620
This feels like the clearest real world validation Tesla can point to right now.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.080
Even though it is limited and gradual, it shows the shift from promises to actual rides.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.520
While the autonomy story is moving in Texas, it is also crossing the Atlantic.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.700
Full self-driving is now operating in Europe.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.840
A journalist from the independent has shared their direct drive experience.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.320
This marks Tesla's first big push into a market with very different regulatory expectations.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.640
Road rules and driving cultures vary significantly from those in North America.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.960
It is a live test of whether the vision only approach travels as well as the company hopes.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:30.620
The technology will face varied infrastructure and driver behaviors that North American data may not have prepared it for.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.120
Success here could dramatically expand the addressable market for Tesla's autonomy features.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.860
It will be instructive to watch how the fleet learning loop adapts to these new conditions.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.380
Speaking of putting hardware in new real world situations.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.460
Tesla is sending its humanoid optimist robot to the finish line of the Boston Marathon.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.700
This is instead of keeping it confined to controlled demonstrations.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.640
The appearance is meant to build public familiarity with humanoid robots.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.220
It moves the technology beyond factories or warehouses into spaces where regular people gather.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.400
Early signals like this suggest how Tesla wants to introduce the robots to everyday life.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.800
Public reactions at a major event like the Boston Marathon could inform future rollout strategies.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.740
It adds a human interest element to robotics ambitions that often feel abstract.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.540
That public debut ties nicely into something we are calling first principles this week.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:28.140
Most conversation around optimists treats the software and AI as the only real challenge.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:36.600
The physics of efficient actuators power density and heat dissipation in a human shaped body may actually be the tighter constraint.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.060
Early prototypes show high energy use even for basic movements.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:48.460
The gap is not just compute, it is lightweight, durable actuation that can run for hours without excessive cooling or battery mass.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:53.760
Human workers bring unmatched adaptability that current machines still only approximate.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.500
As someone with a science background, one see this as the engineering reality check that often gets overlooked.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.520
Tesla's approach draws on the same playbook it used for batteries and cars.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:10.660
That means vertical integration of motors, gearboxes and power electronics combined with massive real world data from deployed units.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.480
If the physics and economics work optimists could ultimately dwarf the vehicle business.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.800
If the constraints prove harder than assumed, it remains a long term research and development project.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:26.060
From robots that might one day work beside us, let us come back to the cars people are buying today.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.340
Ford CEO Jim Farley said he test drove a Xiaomi EV instead of a Tesla.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.300
He felt Tesla does not have an updated vehicle right now.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:40.080
Elon Musk responded by highlighting what he sees as a key limiting factor for foreign companies in China.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.560
The exchange shows how quickly Chinese brands are iterating on both vehicles and technology.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.080
It underscores how central China remains to the entire EV conversation.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.900
Traditional North American executives are now openly using these brands as benchmarks.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.120
For Tesla, this intensifies the need to keep product cycles competitive.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.480
The competitive landscape is clearly shifting in real time.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.360
The Model Y is still posting strong sales in several markets.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:11.740
Fresh competition is arriving with better pricing features and local incentives in different regions.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.500
Segment leadership that once looked comfortable is now being challenged piece by piece.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.360
Customers have more credible choices than they did even a year ago.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.880
Tesla will need to keep refining the product to hold its edge.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.460
This pressure reflects a maturing market where advantages must be earned continuously.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.360
It is a healthy development for buyers even if it complicates life for manufacturers.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.800
Some long-time Tesla owners are expressing real frustration with self-driving promises.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.340
The Wall Street Journal captured stories of people who feel let down by how slowly certain capabilities have matured.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.240
Expectations set years ago have not fully materialized for them.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:53.340
This disappointment is genuine and it can erode trust over time.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:58.860
Tesla continues pushing updates through its fleet learning loop which remains a unique advantage.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:05.060
Still, these owner experiences highlight the human friction that exists alongside the technical progress.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:11.120
The concerns are legitimate and deserve to be taken seriously as regulatory and adoption decisions loom.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:17.940
Tesla is positioned with more real-world miles than anyone else but delivery on timelines will ultimately matter most.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.180
That frustration sits right beside the earnings picture coming up in two days.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.280
Tesla reports earnings on April 22nd.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.280
Analysts are flagging margin pressure from slower deliveries.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:35.680
Conversation on X is split between robotaxi and optimist excitement and near-term realities like inventory and pricing.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.640
The report will reveal how the company balances current margins against heavy investment in autonomy and robotics.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.540
It is a moment where investors will weigh immediate financial health against longer term bets.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.260
The nuance in this earning cycle feels different from previous quarters.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:55.440
While we wait for those numbers, the competitive discounting wars are heating up.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.260
One rival brand is using substantial discounts to target current Tesla owners.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:05.080
These are people who already understand electric vehicles and might switch for the right price.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:11.240
It reflects a maturing market where brand and technology advantages face aggressive pricing pressure.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.800
Legacy leads are no longer automatic in this environment.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.760
Customers could benefit from better deals as companies fight for market share.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:23.680
Tesla will likely need to respond with its own product refreshes or pricing discipline.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.260
And if that was not enough noise, there is one more story that feels pretty off brand.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:32.820
Elon Musk has publicly scorned shady tax loopholes.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.600
Reuters reports that offshore tax strategies likely saved Tesla hundreds of millions.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.420
The story examines the gap between those public statements and standard global corporate tax practices.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.060
Even disruptive companies must navigate the real rules of international business.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.220
It is a reminder that consistency can be difficult in complex operational environments.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.920
For Tesla's business environment, this adds nuance to how the company is perceived.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.080
Before we go keep an eye on how sentiment shifts tomorrow as we draw closer to the earnings release on Wednesday.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.540
That's your Tesla news for today.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.600
TSLA closed at $400.62 down 47 cents 0.1%.

